{"ts": "2026-09-19T23:55:35-05:00", "branch": "main", "commit": "381bad3", "agent": "unknown", "event": "decision", "event_type": "decision", "decision_title": "SPEC 6 projects a node with metadata to a wrapper object, not sibling keys", "decision_reason": "Sibling keys (\"<key>$confidence\") cannot express metadata on an array element -- SPEC 5 makes an array element a node, but a sibling needs a parent key to hang from. They also collide: a literal t$confidence key clashes with the projection of 't: 1 ~0.5', and the collision is manufactured by the projection so SPEC 3's duplicate-key rule never sees it. And SPEC 6 contradicted itself -- it claimed the projection was reconstructable while the shape the corpus enforced discarded confidence and annotations entirely. The wrapper is keyed on the NODE, so object values and array elements behave identically, $-prefixed keys are reserved so nothing collides, and it round-trips. It also matches the precedent $synthesize already set: a distinguishing object, never a bare value.", "summary": "Decision: SPEC 6 projects a node with metadata to a wrapper object, not sibling keys", "rejected_alternatives": [{"option": "sibling keys as originally specified", "reason": ""}, {"option": "keep value-only and drop SPEC 6's round-trip claim", "reason": ""}], "outcome": "Only 2 of 8 existing valid vectors changed; grammar untouched so format stays 1.0. Rust 13/13, Python 16/16.", "supersedes": "", "durability": "strategic", "confidence": "adopted", "entities": [], "artifacts": [], "freshness": "", "expires_after": "", "derived_from": [], "tension": [], "values": [], "domain_owner": "", "stability": "", "event_id": "931f54b1fad9"}
{"ts": "2026-09-19T23:55:35-05:00", "branch": "main", "commit": "381bad3", "agent": "unknown", "event": "decision", "event_type": "decision", "decision_title": "Settle the SPEC 6 gap BEFORE writing more language implementations", "decision_reason": "Every new parser inherits an ambiguous contract. Fixing it at two implementations (Rust + Python) is far cheaper than at four. Vindicated immediately: the new conformance vectors exposed two real bugs in the Rust reference that had been invisible because the old projection discarded the evidence.", "summary": "Decision: Settle the SPEC 6 gap BEFORE writing more language implementations", "rejected_alternatives": [], "outcome": "Found: @cson attaching to the following node instead of setting document version; array elements dropping trailing annotations.", "supersedes": "", "durability": "tactical", "confidence": "verified", "entities": [], "artifacts": [], "freshness": "", "expires_after": "", "derived_from": [], "tension": [], "values": [], "domain_owner": "", "stability": "", "event_id": "c142d9e93fee"}
{"ts": "2026-09-19T23:55:36-05:00", "branch": "main", "commit": "381bad3", "agent": "unknown", "event": "decision", "event_type": "decision", "decision_title": "Language implementations live in impl/<lang>/ in this repo, not separate repos", "decision_reason": "The conformance corpus is the contract (SPEC 8). Keeping parsers beside it means tests reference vectors by relative path and one checkout can run every implementation, so a parser cannot silently drift from the corpus. SPEC's 'cson-py' phrasing hints at separate repos; that remains reversible -- the layout moves cleanly.", "summary": "Decision: Language implementations live in impl/<lang>/ in this repo, not separate repos", "rejected_alternatives": [{"option": "separate cson-py / cson-js repos", "reason": ""}], "outcome": "", "supersedes": "", "durability": "tactical", "confidence": "proposed", "entities": [], "artifacts": [], "freshness": "", "expires_after": "", "derived_from": [], "tension": [], "values": [], "domain_owner": "", "stability": "", "event_id": "401cc5e3c2ac"}
{"ts": "2026-09-19T23:55:50-05:00", "branch": "main", "commit": "381bad3", "agent": "unknown", "event": "invariant", "summary": "Absent confidence is NOT 1.0 (SPEC 4.2). Absent means 'no claim'; every parser must keep the two distinguishable, including in the JSON projection.", "tag": "conformance", "entities": [], "event_id": "813172094c4b"}
{"ts": "2026-09-19T23:55:51-05:00", "branch": "main", "commit": "381bad3", "agent": "unknown", "event": "invariant", "summary": "The conformance corpus is the contract (SPEC 8). A parser is conforming iff it passes every vector; a grammar or projection change means updating the corpus and EVERY implementation in the same commit.", "tag": "conformance", "entities": [], "event_id": "d5236629f9c1"}
{"ts": "2026-09-19T23:55:51-05:00", "branch": "main", "commit": "381bad3", "agent": "unknown", "event": "invariant", "summary": "Keys beginning with '$' are reserved in a projected object, so a document key can never collide with a wrapper member ($value / $confidence / $annotations / $synthesize).", "tag": "spec", "entities": [], "event_id": "7a776400947a"}
{"ts": "2026-09-19T23:55:51-05:00", "branch": "main", "commit": "381bad3", "agent": "unknown", "event": "trap", "summary": "A conformance corpus can fail to constrain the thing it looks like it constrains. Before this session the corpus dropped confidence from its projection, so a parser collapsing absent-confidence to 1.0 passed EVERY vector while violating SPEC 4.2. Fixed by the confidence_absent_vs_one vector -- but the general lesson stands: when adding a spec rule, add a vector that FAILS without it.", "tag": "spec", "entities": [], "event_id": "ea3b93be5876"}
{"ts": "2026-09-19T23:55:51-05:00", "branch": "main", "commit": "381bad3", "agent": "unknown", "event": "trap", "summary": "The JSON projection lived only in tests/conformance.rs, not in the library -- so the corpus was testing a shape no caller could reach, and CsonDocument::to_json() (serde struct form) was something else again. Three shapes in one repo. Projection now lives in src/project.rs and the harness calls it.", "tag": "rust", "entities": [], "event_id": "697719b448ce"}
{"ts": "2026-09-19T23:55:51-05:00", "branch": "main", "commit": "381bad3", "agent": "unknown", "event": "trap", "summary": "@cson is document metadata (SPEC 2.5), not an annotation on the following node. The Rust parser pushed it onto pending_annotations unconditionally; invisible until the projection stopped discarding annotations.", "tag": "parser", "entities": [], "event_id": "4a20b814f60a"}
{"ts": "2026-09-19T23:55:51-05:00", "branch": "main", "commit": "381bad3", "agent": "unknown", "event": "trap", "summary": "An array element is a NODE (SPEC 5), so it may carry confidence and annotations. The Rust array loop hardcoded 'annotations: vec![]', leaving a trailing @note unconsumed for the next iteration to misparse as a value.", "tag": "parser", "entities": [], "event_id": "6fe290f326a1"}
{"ts": "2026-09-19T23:55:51-05:00", "branch": "main", "commit": "381bad3", "agent": "unknown", "event": "pattern", "summary": "Validate a parser against a real document, not just the corpus. The 13 toy vectors did not exercise content-addressed keys, stacked annotations, @corpus(1.0) args or long escaped strings; a 156MB production corpus did, at ~10MB/s.", "tag": "testing", "entities": [], "event_id": "c836e83d8d72"}
{"ts": "2026-09-19T23:56:23-05:00", "branch": "main", "commit": "381bad3", "agent": "unknown", "event": "state_update", "summary": "Format v1.0 draft, grammar stable. SPEC 6 projection settled on the wrapper rule (381bad3). Two parsers -- Rust reference (src/) and Python (impl/python/) -- both pass the full corpus and produce identical projections. Corpus: 11 valid + 5 invalid vectors.", "event_id": "688dbabe8c17"}
{"ts": "2026-09-19T23:56:24-05:00", "branch": "main", "commit": "381bad3", "agent": "unknown", "event": "handoff", "summary": "SPEC 6 settled on a single wrapper rule and the first non-Rust parser landed. The new vectors immediately exposed two real bugs in the Rust reference (@cson attaching to the next node; array elements dropping annotations), both now fixed. Projection moved out of the test harness into src/project.rs.", "next": ["Parsers for JavaScript/TypeScript then Go, built against the settled contract. A CSON printer (parse-only today). Close from_json round-trip against the wrapper projection. Read SPEC.md then conformance/README.md before changing anything -- the corpus is the contract, and a parser must never diverge from it."], "event_id": "b8658b5a6f3d"}
